      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf21" sourceRef="G_fixmerge" targetRef="CA_fix" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf22" sourceRef="CA_fix" targetRef="Script_pkg_fix" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf23" sourceRef="Script_pkg_fix" targetRef="CA_rerev" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf24" sourceRef="CA_rerev" targetRef="G_addressed" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf25" name="yes" sourceRef="G_addressed" targetRef="G_donemerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf26" name="no" sourceRef="G_addressed" targetRef="G_cap" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf27" name="no — next round" sourceRef="G_cap" targetRef="G_fixmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf28" name="yes — the breaker trips" sourceRef="G_cap" targetRef="X_adjudicate" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf29" sourceRef="X_adjudicate" targetRef="G_donemerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf30" sourceRef="G_donemerge" targetRef="X_complete" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf31" sourceRef="X_complete" targetRef="G_taskmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf32" sourceRef="Script_pkg_final" targetRef="CA_final" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf33" sourceRef="CA_final" targetRef="G_finalfind" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf34" name="yes" sourceRef="G_finalfind" targetRef="CA_finalfix" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf35" name="no" sourceRef="G_finalfind" targetRef="G_finalmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf36" sourceRef="CA_finalfix" targetRef="Script_pkg_fw" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf37" sourceRef="Script_pkg_fw" targetRef="CA_finalrerev" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf38" sourceRef="CA_finalrerev" targetRef="X_finaladj" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf39" sourceRef="X_finaladj" targetRef="G_finalmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf40" sourceRef="G_finalmerge" targetRef="X_rulings" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf41" sourceRef="X_rulings" targetRef="Script_rm" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf42" sourceRef="Script_rm" targetRef="End_X" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_nohuman">
        <bpmn:text>This lane is empty, and that is the finding. No human gate exists anywhere in this phase: "Do not pause to check in with your human partner between tasks." Ambiguities are settled by ruling and recorded in the ledger — "a wrong ruling costs rework your human partner can see and undo; a session parked on a question costs their whole day". Exactly four things stop the run and ask: an irreversible or destructive operation, a security-sensitive action, a side effect outside this worktree, and a plan so broken every path forward is a guess. None of them is a step — they can fire anywhere — so nothing draws them.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas1" sourceRef="Note_nohuman" targetRef="Start_X" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_budget">
        <bpmn:text>NO BUDGET MECHANISM. Nothing in superpowers counts tokens, and nothing stops at a threshold — grep the skills for token accounting and there is none. Cost control here is entirely structural: the cheapest model that fits each role, the five-round breaker, one fixer for the whole findings list, no second wave, and review packages handed over as file paths so diffs never enter the controller's context. Every one of those is a fixed shape, not a limit that can be set. For a student on a $50 cap, that is the gap.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas2" sourceRef="Note_budget" targetRef="CA_final" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_noprogram">
        <bpmn:text>Programs do the mechanical work on this plane: resolving the workspace, extracting each task's brief, packaging every diff, deleting the workspace. They produce files, not verdicts. Every decision here is the AI's, including two a script could make: "was that round 5?" is a counter and "tasks left in the plan?" is a ledger lookup. The one machine verdict in this phase, the TDD red/green cycle, happens inside each implementer, a called process with no plane to show it on. The project's own test suite runs one box later, in the parent.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas3" sourceRef="Note_noprogram" targetRef="Script_ws" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_redundancy">
        <bpmn:text>ANTI-REDUNDANT BY DESIGN. One task reviewer per task. One fixer for the whole findings list, never one per finding — "a real session's final-review fix wave cost more than all its tasks combined". One scoped re-review after it, and no second wave. A worker that spawns its own reviewer is "a defect to flag, not rigor". So "how many redundant agents" is a knob superpowers set to 1 and nailed shut: turning it up is a disagreement with the methodology, not a configuration of it.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas4" sourceRef="Note_redundancy" targetRef="CA_finalrerev" />
    </bpmn:subProcess>
    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_finish" name="Finish the branch">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Steps 2 to 6. PLANE NOT YET DRAWN.

Below this box: detect whether this is a normal repo, a named-branch worktree or a detached HEAD; confirm the base branch; present a three-option menu (merge locally, push and open a PR, keep the branch as-is); execute the choice, re-running the suite on the merged result if merging; then clean up the worktree.

Declared in HUMAN because the phase waits on the human's answer to the menu, and the integration decision is explicitly theirs. The agent does the work either side of that answer, which is why this is a plain subprocess with no type marker.

This is also where the controller's "Rulings I made" list surfaces — every decision the agent took on the human's behalf during execution, read at the moment they choose what to do with the branch.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f17</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f18</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:subProcess>
